# Issue and fix

Lead with the decision. Add only what changes the next choice. Yes or no stays yes or no. Leave out how the code works unless they ask. When something cannot be done, stop.

A simple request that is already done, and is not a fix, uses one row. The label is **CONFIRM**. The cell says what has been done. Do not put that request in the four-row table.

```html
<td><strong>CONFIRM</strong></td>
```

A fix uses the four-row table. One command does not make it a confirm. Use the same four rows while exploring and after the change is in the mod. Do not use this table, and do not use an all-caps bold first column, on a reply that is not a fix.

While exploring, **ISSUE** is one summarized accurate statement, such as the craft-count hook is the 3.3 method, so pre-3.3 fails. Not a file-by-file account. **FIX** starts with "Proposed." Use "correct" when that version needs the method that matches it. "Own" means a second method kept beside the other one. **RESULT** is what that suggestion would give. **APPLIED** is No. They use the **FIX** row to offer a cleaner change.

When the change is in the mod, **FIX** states what was done. Do not start that row with "Proposed." **RESULT** is what that change gives. **APPLIED** is Yes.

One HTML table per issue. The first column is the label, all caps and bold, top-left aligned. Those words are not column headers. Leave out how the code does it. In a cell, ordered steps are a numbered list. A simple list is bullets. Columns do not need to be the same width.

When more than one issue is in the reply, each issue is its own table. A title above that table names which issue it is. Leave a gap between tables. Do not stack several issues in one table.

Do not center the first column. Do not set its width. Do not retry `valign`, `vertical-align`, `<center>`, Custom UI Style, or another reload for this.

```html
<td><strong>ISSUE</strong></td>
```

Each four-row table has its own **ISSUE**, **FIX**, **RESULT**, and **APPLIED** rows. The reply that applies a fix shows that table. Leave an earlier table out of a later reply once the user has moved on.

Yes means the change is in the mod. If the mod folder in scope is unclear, ask. Default scope is this workspace. When they say Doom, scope is the Doom areas. Copying a file to a server is not what makes Applied Yes or No.

Doom dedicated-server mod testing only: when Applied is Yes and they still have to copy that file onto the server, end the **APPLIED** cell with "Ready to copy:" and the path. Do not use that line for other projects. Do not turn a missing server copy into No.

## Do not

- Collapse a fix into **CONFIRM** because it was one command.
- Use the four-row table for a simple request that is already done.
- Use this table on an ordinary reply that is not walking through a fix.
- Put **ISSUE**, **FIX**, **RESULT**, or **APPLIED** in the header row.
- Stack more than one issue inside a single table.
- Describe a proposal as if it is already in the mod.
- Name DLL project source files in **FIX** or **APPLIED**.
- Add a **MOD FILES** row or a **NEXT** row.
- Use "Ready to copy" outside Doom dedicated-server mod testing.
- Call it No because the dedicated server or Doom Test does not have the file yet.
